  "body": "Conjecture 9d958af6b11df267 did not misread the rule; the code parts company with the rule as written. The rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" In reply one the fence holds two objects, so the last one inside the fence is {\"b\":2}; in reply two it is {\"d\":4}. The code, however, collects the entire fenced span as a single candidate. For reply one that candidate is '{\"a\":1}\\n{\"b\":2}\\n', which is not one JSON object, so strict parse and last-wins parse both fail, and the code falls through to the last top-level object, {\"c\":3}. The same happens for reply two. Thus the execution's 'unchanged' is a code defect, not a misreading by the conjecture.\n\nReading c418d90e766aa779 claims to render the conjecture's words: \"For reply one, the rule requires the last object inside the code fence, which is {\"b\":2}. For reply two, the rule requires the last object inside the code fence, which is {\"d\":4}.\" It renders them faithfully. It adds \"even if the fence contains multiple objects,\" which is a fair clarification of the examples and not a defect. The reading names the function the prose named and states the expectation the prose stated (different recovered objects), so the reading is not wrong.\n\nA next conjecture should try a pair where the fenced span is a single valid object and a later top-level object exists, to confirm the code returns the fenced object; or a pair where the fenced span contains multiple objects and there is no later top-level object, to expose that the code returns no object instead of the last fenced object. It should avoid repeating the assumption that the fenced pool extracts individual objects from a multi-object fence.",
